{"name":"Ballet White","hex":"#f2e7d8","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ballet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A classical form of dance.","A theatrical presentation of such dancing, usually with music, sometimes in the form of a story."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To perform an action reminiscent of ballet dancing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ballet"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ballet","blocks":[{"text":"Ballet is a type of performance dance that originated during the Italian Renaissance in the fifteenth century and later developed into a concert dance form in France and Russia. It has since become a widespread and highly technical form of dance with its own vocabulary. Ballet has been influential globally and has defined the foundational techniques which are used in many other dance genres and cultures. Various schools around the world have incorporated their own cultures. As a result, ballet has evolved in distinct ways."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Edgar_Degas_-_La_Classe_de_danse.jpg/330px-Edgar_Degas_-_La_Classe_de_danse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballet","wikidata":"Q41425"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect 